// Test: bugfixes/ssr_integer_add_in_call_arg_not_routed
// Regression: 0.30.377 plugin-breakage (Step 3.5 over-routed)
//
// The Step 3.5 call-arg walker (commit b17bffa6) used the bare
// `is_string_concat_chain` predicate to decide whether to rewrite a
// chain inside a Call/MethodCall argument. That predicate matches
// ANY `BinaryOp::Add`, including integer addition — and every
// framework plugin uses integer Add in call args (e.g. `range(0, n+1)`,
// `list.get(arr, i+1)`, etc.). The rewriter converted these to
// `string_concat_transient(string, string) -> string` calls and
// the typechecker rejected them with:
//
//   error[SEM001]: Cannot unify types: integer and string
//
// All 9 framework plugins failed to build on 0.30.377. The fix
// (Step 3.6, this test's gate) tightens the predicate to require at
// least one string-literal leaf in the chain. Integer chains have no
// string literal leaves and are now correctly left alone.
//
// This test exercises three integer-Add-in-call-arg shapes that the
// rewriter MUST NOT touch:
//
//   1. Integer add as the argument to a void Call (printl):
//        printl(i + 1)
//      — pre-3.6 this rewrote `i + 1` to
//        `string_concat_transient(i, 1)`, breaking typecheck.
//
//   2. Integer add as a method-call argument:
//        list.add(items, i + 10)
//      — pre-3.6 same failure.
//
//   3. Integer add as the receiver of a method that takes integer
//      (no string-typed method on integer that uses this shape — we
//      verify via plain integer arithmetic inside the loop body).
//
// All three must compile cleanly and produce the expected loop
// iteration count.
//
// Expected output:
//   5

start:
	integer i = 0
	while i < 5
		// Shape 1: integer add as Call arg.
		// printl on integer
		printl((i + 1).toString())
		i = i + 1
